🔻 Inventors / Creators Who Lost IP Cases in Court
(Based on available search results; citations included.)

1. O’Reilly v. Morse (1853)
Inventor who lost: Samuel Morse
What happened:  
Morse attempted to claim an extremely broad patent over the idea of using electromagnetism for communication.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court invalidated his broad method claims as an unpatentable abstract idea.
Why this counts:  
Morse lost protection over the broadest and most valuable part of his invention.

2. Rubber-Tip Pencil Co. v. Howard (1874)
Inventor who lost: Howard
What happened:  
Howard claimed a patent on attaching an eraser to a pencil.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court ruled that an idea itself is not patentable, only a specific device.
Why this counts:  
The inventor lost because the court said the claimed invention was too conceptual.

3. Egbert v. Lippmann (1881)
Inventor who lost: Samuel Barnes (through his assignee)
What happened:  
Barnes invented a new corset spring but allowed a friend to use it privately.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court held that this counted as public use, invalidating the patent.
Why this counts:  
The inventor lost IP rights because the court ruled the invention was effectively public before filing.

4. Schillinger v. United States (1894)
Inventor who lost: Schillinger
What happened:  
Schillinger claimed the U.S. government infringed his concrete pavement patent.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court ruled he could not sue the U.S. for patent infringement.
Why this counts:  
The inventor lost outright—not because the invention wasn’t mirrored, but because the law barred the claim.

5. Helsinn Healthcare v. Teva (2019)
Inventor who lost: Helsinn (pharmaceutical innovator)
What happened:  
Helsinn sold its invention confidentially before filing.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court ruled that even a confidential sale triggers the “on‑sale bar,” invalidating the patent.
Why this counts:  
The inventor lost patent rights due to procedural rules, despite having created the invention.

6. Minerva Surgical v. Hologic (2021)
Inventor who lost: The original assignor (Minerva’s founder)
What happened:  
The inventor tried to challenge the validity of a patent he had previously assigned.
Court ruling:  
The Supreme Court upheld assignor estoppel, limiting the inventor’s ability to contest the patent.
Why this counts:  
The inventor lost because the law prevented him from defending himself.

